  <section class="lab-levers-section" id="execution-levers">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The diagnostic scorecard</div>
      <h2>Eight execution levers shape the buyer conversation.</h2>
      <div class="lab-levers-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Relevance</h3><p>Does the seller connect the conversation to this buyer, role, company, and moment?</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Opening</h3><p>Does the first minute earn attention, permission, and enough curiosity to continue?</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Listening</h3><p>Does the seller hear the words, intent, hesitation, and business meaning behind the response?</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Questions</h3><p>Do questions uncover useful context, consequences, priorities, process, and decision conditions?</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Value</h3><p>Can the seller translate the offer into a business reason that matters to the buyer?</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Objections</h3><p>Can the seller stay composed, explore resistance, and respond without arguing or collapsing?</p></article>
        <article><span>07</span><h3>Qualification</h3><p>Does the team distinguish a good conversation from an opportunity worth pursuing?</p></article>
        <article><span>08</span><h3>Next Step</h3><p>Does every meaningful interaction end with clear ownership, timing, and purpose?</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-lever-conclusion">The scorecard creates a shared coaching language. Feedback becomes specific enough to practice and measure.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

