  <section class="lab-quiet-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Quiet Power under pressure</div>
      <h2>Control is calm, not loud.</h2>
      <p>Strong sellers do not rush to fill silence, argue with resistance, dump features, or chase approval. They stay present. They hear what changed. They ask the next useful question. They guide the conversation without forcing it.</p>
      <p>Quiet Power is the human layer inside the Lab: composure, clarity, confidence, restraint, and commercial courage when the conversation becomes uncomfortable.</p>
      <div class="lab-quiet-code">
        <span>Listen before reacting</span>
        <span>Clarify before defending</span>
        <span>Diagnose before pitching</span>
        <span>Confirm before advancing</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-failure-section" id="why-sales-training-fails">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why sales training fails</div>
      <h2>A strong workshop can still disappear by Monday.</h2>
      <p class="lab-section-intro">Training loses value when the idea never becomes an observable field behavior. Four breakdowns are especially common.</p>
      <div class="lab-failure-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>No field evidence</h3><p>The program teaches a broad method without inspecting the calls, messages, buyers, and deals where the team is struggling.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>No practice under pressure</h3><p>Sellers understand the concept in a calm room but have not rehearsed the objection, interruption, silence, or uncertainty that changes their behavior.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>No manager standard</h3><p>Managers give conflicting feedback because the company never defined what a strong opening, discovery, qualification decision, or next step looks like.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>No reinforcement loop</h3><p>The team returns to old habits because nobody reviews new evidence, recognizes improvement, corrects drift, or sets the next practice priority.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-failure-conclusion">The cure is not more content. It is a tighter connection between the skill, the practice, the live application, and the next coaching decision.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-manager-section" id="manager-reinforcement">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">Sales manager reinforcement</div>
      <h2>The manager keeps the training alive after the session ends.</h2>
      <p>A sales training company can introduce the language and practice. Daily leadership determines whether it becomes the team standard. Managers need a simple way to hear the same evidence, coach the same levers, and choose one useful behavior at a time.</p>
      <p>Sales Execution Lab can help managers run cleaner call reviews, ask better coaching questions, separate pipeline inspection from skill coaching, and give feedback the seller can apply on the next call.</p>
      <div class="lab-manager-rail">
        <div><span>Observe</span><strong>Use evidence, not impressions.</strong></div>
        <div><span>Coach</span><strong>Focus on one behavior that matters.</strong></div>
        <div><span>Apply</span><strong>Define the next field experiment.</strong></div>
        <div><span>Reinforce</span><strong>Review what changed and repeat.</strong></div>
      </div>
      <p class="lab-manager-note">For distributed teams, virtual sales training and online practice make reinforcement easier to schedule. The standard still has to show up in real calls, messages, follow-up, and pipeline reviews.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label">The coaching process</div>
      <h2>Diagnose → define → practice → apply → inspect → reinforce.</h2>
      <div class="steps lab-process-steps">
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div><h3>Diagnose the Current Execution</h3><p>Review roles, goals, calls, messages, objections, opportunities, pipeline behavior, and manager observations to locate the highest-value gaps.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div><h3>Define What Good Looks Like</h3><p>Create a practical standard for the specific behavior: the opening, question, objection response, follow-up, qualification decision, or next step.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div><h3>Build the Language and Practice</h3><p>Develop talk tracks, message options, coaching examples, role-play scenarios, and repetition around situations the team actually faces.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div><h3>Apply It in Live Selling</h3><p>Take the skill into real outreach, calls, discovery, follow-up, and active opportunities while the new behavior is still deliberate.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div><h3>Inspect the New Evidence</h3><p>Review what changed in seller behavior, buyer response, conversation quality, next steps, and opportunity movement.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="step-num">6</div><div><h3>Reinforce What Works</h3><p>Tighten the language, remove weak habits, give managers a repeatable standard, and move to the next highest-value execution lever.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

